    "title": "“A little lower than the angels” — the Hebrew prints the noun the KJV renders “God” at Genesis 1:1",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Psalm 8:5 has man “a little lower than the angels”. The Leningrad Hebrew prints in that place the noun the KJV renders “God” at Genesis 1:1.",
    "text": "KJV Psalms 8:5: “For thou hast made him a little lower than the angels, and hast crowned him with glory and honour.” Hebrew (WLC) 8:6: וַתְּחַסְּרֵ֣הוּ מְּ֭עַט מֵאֱלֹהִ֑ים [and thou hast made him lack / a little / from (the noun printed here)]. For the same noun, KJV Genesis 1:1: “In the beginning God created”. Hebrew (WLC) Genesis 1:1: בָּרָ֣א אֱלֹהִ֑ים. Brenton’s Greek 8:6: Ἠλάττωσας αὐτὸν βραχύ τι παρʼ ἀγγέλους. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/PSA008.htm) prints: “Thou madest him a little less than angels”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/19/8/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/1/1/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/PSA008.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The English word in the KJV is “angels”. The Hebrew word printed in that place is the noun the KJV renders “God” at Genesis 1:1. The two English words are different words. The Greek prints ἀγγέλους, which Brenton’s English renders “angels”, so the Greek agrees with the KJV and the difference is between the KJV and the Hebrew.",
    "scope": "T applies at Psalms 8:5 (Hebrew and Greek 8:6); the Hebrew edition quoted is the differing text. The Septuagint agrees with the KJV here and is not a differing witness. The record does not say that the Hebrew noun cannot be used of beings other than God, and does not say that “angels” is excluded as a reading; it states which English word the KJV prints, which Hebrew noun is printed, and how the KJV renders that noun at Genesis 1:1. No lexicon is supplied. The quotation of this verse in the New Testament was not compared. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The passage is verse; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the speaker meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew and the Greek number this verse 6 because they count the psalm’s title as verse 1. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
