    "title": "“To know madness and folly” — the Greek has “parables and understanding”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Ecclesiastes 1:17 says the Preacher gave his heart “to know wisdom, and to know madness and folly”. Brenton’s Greek prints a pair of words that Brenton’s English renders “parables and understanding” where the KJV has “madness and folly”.",
    "text": "KJV Ecclesiastes 1:17: “And I gave my heart to know wisdom, and to know madness and folly: I perceived that this also is vexation of spirit.” Hebrew (WLC) 1:17: לָדַ֣עַת חָכְמָ֔ה וְדַ֥עַת הֹולֵלֹ֖ות וְשִׂכְל֑וּת. Brenton’s Greek 1:17: σοφίαν καὶ γνῶσιν, παραβολὰς καὶ ἐπιστήμην. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ECC01.htm) prints: “wisdom, and knowledge, parables and understanding”. For comparison, Brenton’s Greek 2:12: σοφίαν καὶ παραφορὰν καὶ ἀφροσύνην, which Brenton’s English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ECC02.htm) prints as “wisdom, and madness, and folly”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/21/1/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ECC01.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ECC02.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV sets “wisdom” beside “madness and folly”, two things it names as what the heart was given to know. The Greek names “wisdom, and knowledge” and then “parables and understanding”, with nothing that Brenton’s English renders “madness” or “folly”. The same Greek edition prints, at 2:12, a word that its English renders “madness” and another it renders “folly” (“wisdom, and madness, and folly”), so the 1:17 wording is a difference within the Greek book as well as from the KJV.",
    "scope": "T applies at Ecclesiastes 1:17. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is reflective prose and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
