    "summary": "The KJV of Ecclesiastes 10:1 says “so doth a little folly him that is in reputation for wisdom and honour”, after the dead flies. Brenton’s Greek ends with a clause that Brenton’s English renders “a little wisdom is more precious than great glory of folly”.",
    "text": "KJV Ecclesiastes 10:1: “Dead flies cause the ointment of the apothecary to send forth a stinking savour: so doth a little folly him that is in reputation for wisdom and honour.” Hebrew (WLC) 10:1: יָקָ֛ר מֵחָכְמָ֥ה מִכָּבֹ֖וד סִכְל֥וּת מְעָֽט. Brenton’s Greek 10:1: τίμιον ὀλίγον σοφίας ὑπὲρ δόξαν ἀφροσύνης μεγάλην. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ECC10.htm) prints: “a little wisdom is more precious than great glory of folly”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/21/10/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ECC10.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV the second half says that a little folly does harm to a man who has a name for wisdom and honour, as the dead flies do to the ointment. In the Greek the second half says that a little wisdom is more precious than great glory of folly. So the KJV makes folly the thing that does harm, and the Greek makes wisdom the thing that is valued above folly; the harm done by the flies is also not carried into the second half of the Greek.",
    "scope": "T applies at Ecclesiastes 10:1. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is reflective prose and aphorism; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
