    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Ecclesiastes 10:10 ends “but wisdom is profitable to direct”. Brenton’s Greek ends with a clause that carries a negative, which Brenton’s English renders “in that case skill is of no advantage to a man”.",
    "text": "KJV Ecclesiastes 10:10: “If the iron be blunt, and he do not whet the edge, then must he put to more strength: but wisdom is profitable to direct.” Hebrew (WLC) 10:10: וְיִתְרֹ֥ון הַכְשֵׁ֖יר חָכְמָֽה. Brenton’s Greek 10:10: καὶ περίσσεια τῷ ἀνδρὶ οὐ σοφία. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ECC10.htm) prints: “in that case skill is of no advantage to a man”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/21/10/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ECC10.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s clause is positive: wisdom is profitable. The Greek clause contains the negative οὐ, and Brenton’s English renders it as skill being “of no advantage”. So the two clauses can be read to say opposite things about whether wisdom, or skill, is of advantage. The first half of the verse also differs (“the iron be blunt” in the KJV; Brenton’s English has “the axe-head should fall off”), and the record does not rest on it.",
