    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Ecclesiastes 12:5 says “the grasshopper shall be a burden, and desire shall fail”. Brenton’s Greek has a plant name that Brenton’s English renders “the caper” where the KJV has “desire”, and a different verb for the insect.",
    "text": "KJV Ecclesiastes 12:5: “Also when they shall be afraid of that which is high, and fears shall be in the way, and the almond tree shall flourish, and the grasshopper shall be a burden, and desire shall fail: because man goeth to his long home, and the mourners go about the streets:” Hebrew (WLC) 12:5: וְיִסְתַּבֵּ֣ל הֶֽחָגָ֔ב וְתָפֵ֖ר הָֽאֲבִיֹּונָ֑ה. Brenton’s Greek 12:5: καὶ παχυνθῇ ἡ ἀκρὶς, καὶ διασκεδασθῇ ἡ κάππαρις. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ECC12.htm) prints: “and the locust shall increase, and the caper shall be scattered”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/21/12/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ECC12.htm.",
    "reasoning": "Both texts list things in this passage, and both name an insect. The KJV’s last item is “desire”, an inner state, and says it shall “fail”. The Greek’s last item is a plant, the caper, which “shall be scattered”. The insect also differs in what happens to it: it “shall be a burden” in the KJV and “shall increase” in Brenton’s English. The Hebrew edition prints a noun after the verb that the record quotes and does not gloss.",
