    "title": "“Thy love is better than wine” — the Greek has “thy breasts are better than wine”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of the Song of Solomon 1:2 says “thy love is better than wine”. Brenton’s Greek prints a word that Brenton’s English renders “breasts” where the KJV has “love”; the same difference stands at 4:10 and 7:12.",
    "text": "KJV Song of Solomon 1:2: “Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth: for thy love is better than wine.” Hebrew (WLC) 1:2: כִּֽי־טֹובִ֥ים דֹּדֶ֖יךָ מִיָּֽיִן. Brenton’s Greek 1:2: ὅτι ἀγαθοὶ μαστοί σου ὑπὲρ οἶνον. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/SNG01.htm) prints: “thy breasts are better than wine”. KJV Song of Solomon 4:10: “How fair is thy love, my sister, my spouse! how much better is thy love than wine! and the smell of thine ointments than all spices!” Hebrew (WLC) 4:10: מַה־טֹּ֤בוּ דֹדַ֙יִךְ֙ מִיַּ֔יִן. Brenton’s Greek 4:10: τί ἐκαλλιώθησαν μαστοί σου ἀπὸ οἴνου; Brenton’s English prints “how much more beautiful are thy breasts than wine”. KJV Song of Solomon 7:12: “Let us get up early to the vineyards; let us see if the vine flourish, whether the tender grape appear, and the pomegranates bud forth: there will I give thee my loves.” Hebrew (WLC) 7:13: שָׁ֛ם אֶתֵּ֥ן אֶת־דֹּדַ֖י לָֽךְ. Brenton’s Greek 7:13: ἐκεῖ δώσω τοὺς μαστούς μου σοί; Brenton’s English prints “there will I give thee my breasts”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/22/1/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/22/4/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/22/7/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/SNG01.htm, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/SNG04.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/SNG07.htm.",
    "reasoning": "At each of the three verses the KJV has “love” (or “loves”) and the Greek has a word that Brenton’s English renders “breasts”. At 1:2 the compared thing, wine, is the same in both; at 7:12 the object of the giving differs, “my loves” in the KJV and “my breasts” in the Greek. The Hebrew edition prints a plural noun in each of the three places; the record quotes them and does not say what they mean. So the Greek names a part of the body where the KJV names an affection or its expressions.",
    "scope": "T applies at Song of Solomon 1:2, 4:10 and 7:12; the first verse is the one quoted in full. The KJV 7:12 is numbered 7:13 in the Hebrew and the Greek. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is love poetry; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
