    "text": "KJV Song of Solomon 1:10: “Thy cheeks are comely with rows of jewels, thy neck with chains of gold.” Hebrew (WLC) 1:10: לְחָיַ֙יִךְ֙ בַּתֹּרִ֔ים צַוָּארֵ֖ךְ בַּחֲרוּזִֽים. Brenton’s Greek 1:10: σιαγόνες σου ὡς τρυγόνος, τράχηλός σου ὡς ὁρμίσκοι. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/SNG01.htm) prints: “thy cheeks beautiful as those of a dove, thy neck as chains”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/22/1/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/SNG01.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV the cheeks are adorned with “rows of jewels”, and the neck with chains of gold. In the Greek the cheeks are likened to “those of a dove”, a comparison with a bird, and the neck to “chains”, with no gold named. The Hebrew edition prints one noun after the preposition in the first clause; the record quotes it and does not say what it means, so what is shown is that the KJV names ornaments where the Greek names a bird.",
