    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of the Song of Solomon 2:7 and 3:5 has the adjuration “by the roes, and by the hinds of the field”. Brenton’s Greek prints two words that Brenton’s English renders “the powers” and “the virtues” where the KJV has two animals.",
    "text": "KJV Song of Solomon 2:7: “I charge you, O ye daughters of Jerusalem, by the roes, and by the hinds of the field, that ye stir not up, nor awake my love, till he please.” Hebrew (WLC) 2:7: בִּצְבָאֹ֔ות אֹ֖ו בְּאַיְלֹ֣ות הַשָּׂדֶ֑ה. Brenton’s Greek 2:7: ἐν δυνάμεσι καὶ ἐν ἰσχύσεσι τοῦ ἀγροῦ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/SNG02.htm) prints: “by the powers and by the virtues of the field”. KJV Song of Solomon 3:5: “I charge you, O ye daughters of Jerusalem, by the roes, and by the hinds of the field, that ye stir not up, nor awake my love, till he please.” Brenton’s Greek 3:5: ἐν ταῖς δυνάμεσι, καὶ ἐν ταῖς ἰσχύσεσι τοῦ ἀγροῦ; Brenton’s English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/SNG03.htm) prints “by the powers and by the virtues of the field”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/22/2/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/SNG02.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/SNG03.htm.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV the people addressed are made to swear by two animals, “the roes” and “the hinds”, of the field. In the Greek they are made to swear by “the powers” and “the virtues” of the field, which Brenton’s English renders as abstract nouns. So what the oath is by differs between the two texts: animals in the KJV and powers in the Greek. Both texts then forbid stirring up or awakening love until it please.",
    "scope": "T applies at Song of Solomon 2:7 and 3:5. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is love poetry; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the writer meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
