    "summary": "The KJV of the Song of Solomon 6:4 says “Thou art beautiful, O my love, as Tirzah”. Brenton’s Greek prints a word that Brenton’s English renders “Pleasure” where the KJV has the name Tirzah.",
    "text": "KJV Song of Solomon 6:4: “Thou art beautiful, O my love, as Tirzah, comely as Jerusalem, terrible as an army with banners.” Hebrew (WLC) 6:4: כְּתִרְצָ֔ה. Brenton’s Greek 6:4: ὡς εὐδοκία. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/SNG06.htm) prints: “as Pleasure”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/22/6/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/SNG06.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV prints a proper name, Tirzah, set beside Jerusalem in the second clause. Brenton’s Greek prints the word εὐδοκία, which Brenton’s English renders “Pleasure” with a capital letter, and keeps the name Jerusalem in the next clause. So the first term of the comparison is a name in the KJV and a common noun in the Greek; the Greek does not show that Tirzah is not a place, and the KJV does not show that the Hebrew name is not also a word.",
