    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Jeremiah 1:17 says “be not dismayed at their faces, lest I confound thee before them”. Brenton’s Greek prints words that Brenton’s English renders “be not afraid of their face, neither be thou alarmed before them; for I am with thee”.",
    "text": "KJV Jeremiah 1:17: “be not dismayed at their faces, lest I confound thee before them”. Hebrew (WLC) 1:17: תֵּחַת֙ מִפְּנֵיהֶ֔ם פֶּֽן־אֲחִתְּךָ֖ לִפְנֵיהֶֽם. Brenton’s Greek 1:17: μὴ φοβηθῇς ἀπὸ προσώπου αὐτῶν, μηδὲ πτοηθῇς ἐναντίον αὐτῶν, ὅτι μετὰ σοῦ εἰμι. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/JER01.htm) prints: “be not afraid of their face, neither be thou alarmed before them; for I am with thee”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/24/1/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JER01.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV gives a reason for not being dismayed that is a warning: “lest I confound thee before them”. The Greek in Brenton’s English gives a reason that is a promise: “for I am with thee”. The clause that follows the command is a threat in the KJV and an assurance in Brenton’s English.",
    "scope": "T applies at Jeremiah 1:17. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is prophecy in verse and sermon; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the prophet meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
