    "summary": "The KJV of Jeremiah 29:2 says “and the carpenters, and the smiths”. Brenton’s Greek prints words that Brenton’s English renders “and every freeman, and bondman, and artificer”.",
    "text": "KJV Jeremiah 29:2: “and the carpenters, and the smiths”. Hebrew (WLC) 29:2: וְהֶחָרָ֥שׁ וְהַמַּסְגֵּ֖ר. Brenton’s Greek 36:2: καὶ παντὸς ἐλευθέρου, καὶ δεσμώτου, καὶ τεχνίτου. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/JER36.htm) prints: “and every freeman, and bondman, and artificer”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/24/29/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JER36.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV lists two trades among those who left Jerusalem with Jeconiah: “the carpenters, and the smiths”. Brenton’s English lists “every freeman, and bondman, and artificer”: three classes, one of them a craftsman. The list is of trades in the KJV and of social classes in Brenton’s English.",
    "scope": "T applies at Jeremiah 29:2. Brenton’s Greek prints this verse in its chapter 36, as verse 2, because it places the book’s chapters in a different order from the Hebrew and the KJV. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is prophecy in verse and sermon; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the prophet meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
