    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Jeremiah 32:17 and 32:27 asks and says that nothing is “too hard” for the Lord. Brenton’s Greek prints a verb that Brenton’s English renders “hidden” at both verses.",
    "text": "KJV Jeremiah 32:17: “there is nothing too hard for thee”. Hebrew (WLC) 32:17: לֹֽא־יִפָּלֵ֥א מִמְּךָ֖. Brenton’s Greek 39:17: οὐ μὴ ἀποκρυβῇ ἀπὸ σοῦ οὐθὲν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/JER39.htm) prints: “nothing can be hidden from thee”. KJV Jeremiah 32:27: “is there any thing too hard for me?”. Hebrew (WLC) 32:27: יִפָּלֵ֖א כָּל־דָּבָֽר. Brenton’s Greek 39:27: μὴ ἀπʼ ἐμοῦ κρυβήσεταί τι. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/JER39.htm) prints: “shall anything be hidden from me”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/24/32/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JER39.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s word, “too hard”, is about what can be done. The Greek in Brenton’s English, “hidden”, is about what can be concealed. The same Hebrew verb stands at both verses in the Leningrad text and the Greek verb of concealment stands at both in Brenton’s Greek.",
    "scope": "T applies at Jeremiah 32:17 and Jeremiah 32:27. Brenton’s Greek places the book’s chapters in a different order from the Hebrew and the KJV; here 32:17 is printed at 39:17 and 32:27 is printed at 39:27. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is prophecy in verse and sermon; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the prophet meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
