    "mode": "contradictions",
    "ref": "Jeremiah 31:8",
    "appliesTo": [
      "Jeremiah 31:8"
    ],
    "title": "“With them the blind and the lame, the woman with child” — the Greek has “to the feast of the passover”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Jeremiah 31:8 says “gather them from the coasts of the earth, and with them the blind and the lame, the woman with child and her that travaileth with child together”. Brenton’s Greek prints words that Brenton’s English renders “gather them from the end of the earth to the feast of the passover: and the people shall beget a great multitude”.",
    "text": "KJV Jeremiah 31:8: “gather them from the coasts of the earth, and with them the blind and the lame, the woman with child and her that travaileth with child together”. Hebrew (WLC) 31:8: וְקִבַּצְתִּים֮ מִיַּרְכְּתֵי־אָרֶץ֒ בָּ֚ם עִוֵּ֣ר וּפִסֵּ֔חַ הָרָ֥ה וְיֹלֶ֖דֶת יַחְדָּ֑ו. Brenton’s Greek 38:8: συνάξω αὐτοὺς ἀπʼ ἐσχάτου τῆς γῆς ἐν ἑορτῇ φασέκ· καὶ τεκνοποιήσει ὄχλον πολὺν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/JER38.htm) prints: “will gather them from the end of the earth to the feast of the passover: and the people shall beget a great multitude”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/24/31/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JER38.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV lists who is gathered: “the blind and the lame, the woman with child and her that travaileth with child”. The Greek in Brenton’s English has no such list: it has the feast of the passover and a people that “shall beget a great multitude”.",
    "scope": "T applies at Jeremiah 31:8. Brenton’s Greek places the book’s chapters in a different order from the Hebrew and the KJV; here 31:8 is printed at 38:8. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The book is prophecy in verse and sermon; the comparison is of what the texts print, not of what the prophet meant beyond the words quoted. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
