    "title": "“Loruhamah”, “Loammi”, “Ammi”, “Ruhamah”, “Ishi” — the names the KJV leaves untranslated, and the words the Greek prints",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In Hosea 1:6, 1:9, 2:1 and 2:16 the KJV prints the names Loruhamah, Loammi, Ammi, Ruhamah and Ishi. The Hebrew quoted prints each name as ordinary words that appear elsewhere in the same verses, and Brenton’s Greek prints Greek words, which Brenton’s own English gives as “Unpitied”, “Not my people”, “My people”, “Pitied” and “My husband”, where the KJV prints a name.",
    "text": "KJV Hosea 1:6: “Call her name Loruhamah: for I will no more have mercy upon the house of Israel”. Hebrew (WLC) 1:6: לֹ֣א רֻחָ֑מָה … לֹ֨א אֹוסִ֜יף עֹ֗וד אֲרַחֵם֙. Brenton’s Greek 1:6: οὐκ ἠλεημένη … οὐ μὴ προσθήσω ἔτι ἐλεῆσαι. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/HOS01.htm) prints: “Unpitied” … “I will no more have mercy”. KJV Hosea 1:9: “Call his name Loammi: for ye are not my people”. Hebrew (WLC) 1:9: לֹ֣א עַמִּ֑י … אַתֶּם֙ לֹ֣א עַמִּ֔י. Brenton’s Greek 1:9: οὐ λαός μου· διότι ὑμεῖς οὐ λαός μου. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/HOS01.htm) prints: “Not my people: for ye are not my people”. KJV Hosea 2:1: “Say ye unto your brethren, Ammi; and to your sisters, Ruhamah”. Hebrew (WLC) 2:3: עַמִּ֑י … רֻחָֽמָה. Brenton’s Greek 2:3: λαός μου, καὶ τῇ ἀδελφῇ ὑμῶν, ἠλεημένη. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/HOS02.htm) prints: “My people, and to your sister, Pitied”. KJV Hosea 2:16: “thou shalt call me Ishi; and shalt call me no more Baali”. Hebrew (WLC) 2:18: אִישִׁ֑י. Brenton’s Greek 2:18: ὁ ἀνήρ μου. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/HOS02.htm) prints: “My husband”. KJV Hosea 2:7: “I will go and return to my first husband”. Hebrew (WLC) 2:9: אִישִׁ֣י הָֽרִאשֹׁ֔ון. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/28/1/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/28/2/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/HOS01.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/HOS02.htm.",
    "reasoning": "At 1:6 the Hebrew name is the negative word followed by רחמה, and the same verse prints the verb ארחם in the KJV’s “have mercy”. At 1:9 the name is the negative word and עמי, and the same verse prints the same words in “ye are not my people”. At 2:1 the names are עמי and רחמה, the two words of 1:9 and 1:6. At 2:16 the name is אישי, the word the Hebrew of 2:7 prints where the KJV has “my first husband”. The Greek prints ordinary Greek words in each place (Brenton’s own English: “Unpitied”, “Not my people”, “My people”, “Pitied”, “My husband”), not a name. The KJV’s English prints a name in each place.",
    "scope": "T applies at Hosea 1:6, Hosea 1:9, Hosea 2:1 and Hosea 2:16. The record does not give a dictionary meaning for any Hebrew word; it points only to the same Hebrew words printed in the verses quoted. At 2:16 the Greek prints “Baalim” where the KJV prints “Baali”; that is a difference of form and is not the finding. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Hosea’s verse numbers differ between the KJV and the Hebrew text in places (the KJV’s 1:10–2:23 are 2:1–2:25 in the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek); the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The names may be the usual way for the KJV to give names that have meanings; the record does not say that the KJV is wrong, and it does not say the Hebrew of the names is anything other than the words quoted. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
