    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Hosea 5:7 has “a month” devouring them with their portions. Brenton’s Greek prints a word that Brenton’s own English renders “the cankerworm”.",
    "text": "KJV Hosea 5:7: “now shall a month devour them with their portions”. Hebrew (WLC) 5:7: עַתָּ֛ה יֹאכְלֵ֥ם חֹ֖דֶשׁ אֶת־חֶלְקֵיהֶֽם. Brenton’s Greek 5:7: νῦν καταφάγεται αὐτοὺς ἡ ἐρυσίβη, καὶ τοὺς κλήρους αὐτῶν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/HOS05.htm) prints: “now shall the cankerworm devour them and their heritages”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/28/5/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/HOS05.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints חדש after the verb. The Greek prints a different noun, “ἐρυσίβη”, as the thing that devours. The two nouns name different things on their face: a period of time and a pest.",
    "scope": "T applies at Hosea 5:7. The record does not say what the Hebrew word חדש can mean beyond its being the word the KJV renders “a month”. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Hosea’s verse numbers differ between the KJV and the Hebrew text in places (the KJV’s 1:10–2:23 are 2:1–2:25 in the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek); the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
