    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "At Hosea 5:14 and 13:7 the KJV has the Lord “as a lion”, with “a young lion” at 5:14 and “a leopard” at 13:7. Brenton’s Greek prints a word that Brenton’s own English renders “a panther” in the place of the first animal at both verses, and “a lion” at 5:14 for the KJV’s “a young lion”.",
    "text": "KJV Hosea 5:14: “as a lion, and as a young lion to the house of Judah”. Hebrew (WLC) 5:14: כַשַּׁ֙חַל֙ לְאֶפְרַ֔יִם וְכַכְּפִ֖יר לְבֵ֣ית יְהוּדָ֑ה. Brenton’s Greek 5:14: ὡς πανθὴρ τῷ Ἐφραὶμ, καὶ ὡς λέων τῷ οἴκῳ Ἰούδα. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/HOS05.htm) prints: “as a panther to Ephraim, and as a lion to the house of Juda”. KJV Hosea 13:7: “as a lion: as a leopard by the way”. Hebrew (WLC) 13:7: כְּמֹו־שָׁ֑חַל כְּנָמֵ֖ר עַל־דֶּ֥רֶךְ. Brenton’s Greek 13:7: ὡς πανθὴρ, καὶ ὡς πάρδαλις. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/HOS13.htm) prints: “as a panther, and as a leopard”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/28/5/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/28/13/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/HOS05.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/HOS13.htm.",
    "reasoning": "At 13:7 the Hebrew quoted prints שחל at the place of the KJV’s “lion” and the Greek prints “πανθήρ”; at 5:14 the Hebrew prints כשחל and the Greek prints “πανθήρ”. At 5:14 the Greek’s second animal is “λέων”, where the KJV’s second is “a young lion”. The animal names differ at each place.",
    "scope": "T applies at Hosea 5:14 and Hosea 13:7. The record does not say which animal the Hebrew words quoted name, and does not say that the Greek names the same animal as the KJV. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Hosea’s verse numbers differ between the KJV and the Hebrew text in places (the KJV’s 1:10–2:23 are 2:1–2:25 in the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek); the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The demonstrated animal contrast is the first animal, lion versus panther; lion versus young lion does not by itself establish a different animal.",
