    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Hosea 11:1 has “called my son out of Egypt”. Brenton’s Greek prints “τὰ τέκνα αὐτοῦ”, “his children”, as the object. The Greek New Testament of Matthew 2:15 prints “τὸν υἱόν μου”, “my son”.",
    "text": "KJV Hosea 11:1: “called my son out of Egypt”. Hebrew (WLC) 11:1: וּמִמִּצְרַ֖יִם קָרָ֥אתִי לִבְנִֽי. Brenton’s Greek 11:1: ἐξ Αἰγύπτου μετεκάλεσα τὰ τέκνα αὐτοῦ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/HOS11.htm) prints: “out of Egypt have I called his children”. KJV Matthew 2:15: “Out of Egypt have I called my son”. Greek New Testament Matthew 2:15 (Tischendorf, eighth edition): τὸν υἱόν μου. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/28/11/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/HOS11.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/2/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/2/.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints לבני, a singular noun with “my”. The Greek of Hosea prints a plural noun with “his”. The Greek of Matthew 2:15 (Tischendorf’s eighth edition, with the Textus Receptus text served beside it agreeing in these words) prints a singular noun with “my”, as the KJV does at Hosea 11:1 and at Matthew 2:15.",
    "scope": "T applies at Hosea 11:1. The record does not say what the Hebrew word לבני means beyond its being the word the KJV renders “my son”. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Hosea’s verse numbers differ between the KJV and the Hebrew text in places (the KJV’s 1:10–2:23 are 2:1–2:25 in the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek); the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament verse is quoted for its wording only. The record does not examine how Matthew 2:15 uses Hosea 11:1, and does not say which text Matthew drew on. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
