    "title": "“O death, I will be thy plagues; O grave, I will be thy destruction” — the Greek asks “where is thy penalty, O death? … where is thy sting?”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Hosea 13:14 has two statements, “I will be thy plagues” and “I will be thy destruction”. Brenton’s Greek has two questions, “ποῦ ἡ δίκη σου” and “ποῦ τὸ κέντρον σου”, which Brenton’s own English gives as “where is thy penalty” and “where is thy sting”. The KJV of 1 Corinthians 15:55 asks “where is thy sting?”.",
    "text": "KJV Hosea 13:14: “O death, I will be thy plagues; O grave, I will be thy destruction”. Hebrew (WLC) 13:14: אֱהִ֨י דְבָרֶיךָ֜ מָ֗וֶת אֱהִ֤י קָֽטָבְךָ֙ שְׁאֹ֔ול. Brenton’s Greek 13:14: ποῦ ἡ δίκη σου θάνατε; ποῦ τὸ κέντρον σου ᾅδη. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/HOS13.htm) prints: “where is thy penalty, O death? O Hades, where is thy sting?”. KJV 1 Corinthians 15:55: “O death, where is thy sting? O grave, where is thy victory?”. Greek New Testament 1 Corinthians 15:55 (Tischendorf, eighth edition): ποῦ σου, θάνατε, τὸ κέντρον. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/28/13/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/HOS13.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/46/15/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/46/15/.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints אהי דבריך and אהי קטבך, the wording the KJV renders as statements of what the Lord will be. The Greek words quoted print the question word and a noun in each clause, with δίκη and κέντρον for the nouns. The KJV states what the Lord will be; the Greek asks where the nouns are. The Greek New Testament of 1 Corinthians 15:55 prints the same question word and “τὸ κέντρον” with “θάνατε”, as the KJV’s “where is thy sting”.",
    "scope": "T applies at Hosea 13:14. The record does not say what the Hebrew nouns quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Hosea’s verse numbers differ between the KJV and the Hebrew text in places (the KJV’s 1:10–2:23 are 2:1–2:25 in the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek); the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament verse is quoted for its wording only. The record does not say that 1 Corinthians 15:55 quotes Hosea 13:14 and does not say which text it draws on. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
