    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Hosea 14:2 has “the calves of our lips”. Brenton’s Greek prints “καρπὸν χειλέων ἡμῶν”, “the fruit of our lips”. The KJV of Hebrews 13:15 has “the fruit of our lips”, and its Greek prints “καρπὸν χειλέων”.",
    "text": "KJV Hosea 14:2: “so will we render the calves of our lips”. Hebrew (WLC) 14:3: וּֽנְשַׁלְּמָ֥ה פָרִ֖ים שְׂפָתֵֽינוּ. Brenton’s Greek 14:3: ἀνταποδώσομεν καρπὸν χειλέων ἡμῶν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/HOS14.htm) prints: “we will render in return the fruit of our lips”. KJV Hebrews 13:15: “the fruit of our lips giving thanks to his name”. Greek New Testament Hebrews 13:15 (Tischendorf, eighth edition): καρπὸν χειλέων. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/28/14/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/HOS14.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/58/13/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/58/13/.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints פרים before “our lips”, the noun the KJV renders “calves”. The Greek of Hosea prints “καρπόν”, a noun for fruit. The Greek New Testament of Hebrews 13:15 prints the same two words as the Greek of Hosea, “καρπὸν χειλέων”.",
    "scope": "T applies at Hosea 14:2. The record does not say what the Hebrew word פרים means. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Hosea’s verse numbers differ between the KJV and the Hebrew text in places (the KJV’s 1:10–2:23 are 2:1–2:25 in the Hebrew and Brenton’s Greek); the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament verse is quoted for its wording only. The record does not say that Hebrews 13:15 quotes Hosea 14:2 and does not say which text it draws on. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
