    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "At Daniel 4:8, 4:9, 4:18 and 5:14 the KJV has “the spirit of the holy gods” (and “the spirit of the gods” at 5:14). Brenton’s Greek prints “πνεῦμα Θεοῦ ἅγιον”, which Brenton’s own English gives as “the Holy Spirit of God”, and at 5:14 “πνεῦμα Θεοῦ”.",
    "text": "KJV Daniel 4:8: “in whom is the spirit of the holy gods”. Aramaic (WLC) 4:5: רֽוּחַ־אֱלָהִ֥ין קַדִּישִׁ֖ין. Brenton’s Greek 4:8: πνεῦμα Θεοῦ ἅγιον ἐν ἑαυτῷ ἔχει. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/DAG04.htm) prints: “the Holy Spirit of God”. KJV Daniel 4:9: “the spirit of the holy gods is in thee”. Aramaic (WLC) 4:6: ר֣וּחַ אֱלָהִ֤ין קַדִּישִׁין֙ בָּ֔ךְ. Brenton’s Greek 4:9: πνεῦμα Θεοῦ ἅγιον ἐν σοὶ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/DAG04.htm) prints: “the Holy Spirit of God is in thee”. KJV Daniel 4:18: “the spirit of the holy gods is in thee”. Aramaic (WLC) 4:15: רֽוּחַ־אֱלָהִ֥ין קַדִּישִׁ֖ין בָּֽךְ. Brenton’s Greek 4:18: πνεῦμα Θεοῦ ἅγιον ἐν σοί. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/DAG04.htm) prints: “the Holy Spirit of God is in thee”. KJV Daniel 5:14: “the spirit of the gods is in thee”. Aramaic (WLC) 5:14: ר֥וּחַ אֱלָהִ֖ין בָּ֑ךְ. Brenton’s Greek 5:14: πνεῦμα Θεοῦ ἐν σοί. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/DAG05.htm) prints: “the Spirit of God is in thee”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/27/4/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/27/5/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DAG04.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DAG05.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Aramaic quoted prints אלהין, with קדישין after it at 4:8, 4:9 and 4:18, at each place where the KJV prints “gods”. The Greek prints “Θεοῦ”, the singular genitive of the word for God, at each place, and puts “ἅγιον” after “πνεῦμα Θεοῦ”. The KJV’s “gods” is plural and the Greek’s word is singular.",
    "scope": "T applies at Daniel 4:8, Daniel 4:9, Daniel 4:18 and Daniel 5:14. This part of Daniel is written in Aramaic, and the Aramaic is quoted from the same digital text; the record does not say what the Aramaic words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Daniel’s verse numbers differ between the KJV, the Hebrew and Aramaic text and Brenton’s Greek in places; the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s edition prints this Greek Daniel at the address cited under the title “Daniel (Greek)”. The same edition also prints a second English text titled Daniel, whose Greek page was not found at the address pattern used; the record does not say which Greek form of Daniel the page quoted is, and it does not compare any other Greek form. The record does not say that the Aramaic word cannot refer to one God, and it does not say what the king meant by the words he is quoted as speaking. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
