    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Daniel 8:9 has “a little horn”, growing “toward the south, and toward the east, and toward the pleasant land”. Brenton’s Greek prints “κέρας ἓν ἰσχυρόν”, “one strong horn”, growing “toward the south, and toward the host”.",
    "text": "KJV Daniel 8:9: “a little horn, which waxed exceeding great, toward the south, and toward the east, and toward the pleasant land”. Hebrew (WLC) 8:9: קֶֽרֶן־אַחַ֖ת מִצְּעִירָ֑ה וַתִּגְדַּל־יֶ֛תֶר אֶל־הַנֶּ֥גֶב וְאֶל־הַמִּזְרָ֖ח וְאֶל־הַצֶּֽבִי. Brenton’s Greek 8:9: κέρας ἓν ἰσχυρόν, καὶ ἐμεγαλύνθη περισσῶς πρὸς τὸν Νότον, καὶ πρὸς τὴν δύναμιν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/DAG08.htm) prints: “one strong horn, and it grew very great toward the south, and toward the host”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/27/8/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DAG08.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints מצעירה for the horn and הצבי for the last direction. The Greek prints a word for strong where the KJV has “little”, prints no word for the east, and prints “τὴν δύναμιν”, which Brenton’s own English gives as “the host”, where the KJV has “the pleasant land”.",
    "scope": "T applies at Daniel 8:9. The record does not say what the Hebrew words מצעירה and הצבי mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Daniel’s verse numbers differ between the KJV, the Hebrew and Aramaic text and Brenton’s Greek in places; the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s edition prints this Greek Daniel at the address cited under the title “Daniel (Greek)”. The same edition also prints a second English text titled Daniel, whose Greek page was not found at the address pattern used; the record does not say which Greek form of Daniel the page quoted is, and it does not compare any other Greek form. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
