    "summary": "The KJV of Daniel 12:4 has “many shall run to and fro, and knowledge shall be increased”. Brenton’s Greek prints “ἕως διδαχθῶσι πολλοί”, which Brenton’s own English gives as “until many are taught”.",
    "text": "KJV Daniel 12:4: “many shall run to and fro, and knowledge shall be increased”. Hebrew (WLC) 12:4: יְשֹׁטְט֥וּ רַבִּ֖ים וְתִרְבֶּ֥ה הַדָּֽעַת. Brenton’s Greek 12:4: ἕως διδαχθῶσι πολλοὶ καὶ πληθυνθῇ ἡ γνῶσις. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/DAG12.htm) prints: “until many are taught, and knowledge is increased”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/27/12/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DAG12.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints ישטטו רבים. The KJV has many running to and fro. The Greek has a conjunction of time and a verb for being taught. The verb differs, and the Greek ties it to the time of the end with “until”.",
    "scope": "T applies at Daniel 12:4. The record does not say what the Hebrew verb ישטטו means. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Daniel’s verse numbers differ between the KJV, the Hebrew and Aramaic text and Brenton’s Greek in places; the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s edition prints this Greek Daniel at the address cited under the title “Daniel (Greek)”. The same edition also prints a second English text titled Daniel, whose Greek page was not found at the address pattern used; the record does not say which Greek form of Daniel the page quoted is, and it does not compare any other Greek form. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
