    "id": "between-daniel-3-23-and-3-24-the-greek-page-prints-a-prayer-and-a-song",
    "mode": "contradictions",
    "ref": "Daniel 3:24",
    "appliesTo": [
      "Daniel 3:24"
    ],
    "title": "Between Daniel 3:23 and 3:24 the Greek page prints a prayer and a song — verse labels 24 to 90",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV, Daniel 3:23 (the three men falling bound into the furnace) is followed at once by 3:24, the king’s astonishment. Brenton’s Greek page prints sixty-five verses between them, labelled 24 to 90 (the labels 67 to 70 are not on the page, and 72a and 72b are), beginning with the men walking in the flame and Azarias praying, and prints the king’s astonishment at 91.",
    "text": "KJV Daniel 3:23: “fell down bound into the midst of the burning fiery furnace”. Aramaic (WLC) 3:23: נְפַ֛לוּ לְגֹֽוא־אַתּוּן־נוּרָ֥א יָֽקִדְתָּ֖א מְכַפְּתִֽין. Brenton’s Greek 3:23: ἔπεσον εἰς μέσον τῆς καμίνου τῆς καιομένης πεπεδημένοι. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/DAG03.htm) prints: “fell bound into the midst of the burning furnace”. KJV Daniel 3:24: “Then Nebuchadnezzar the king was astonished, and rose up in haste”. Aramaic (WLC) 3:24: אֱדַ֙יִן֙ נְבוּכַדְנֶצַּ֣ר מַלְכָּ֔א תְּוַ֖הּ וְקָ֣ם בְּהִתְבְּהָלָ֑ה. Brenton’s Greek: 3:91 Καὶ Ναβουχοδονόσορ ἤκουσεν ὑμνούντων αὐτῶν, καὶ ἐθαύμασε, καὶ ἐξανέστη ἐν σπουδῇ … 3:24 καὶ περιεπάτουν ἐν μέσῳ τῆς φλογὸς, ὑμνοῦντες τὸν Θεὸν, καὶ εὐλογοῦντες τὸν Κύριον … 3:25 Καὶ συστὰς Ἀζαρίας προσηύξατο οὕτως. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/DAG03.htm) prints: 3:91 “And Nabuchodonosor heard them singing praises; and he wondered, and rose up in haste” … 3:24 “and walked in the midst of the flame, singing praise to God, and blessing the Lord” … 3:25 “Then Azarias stood up, and prayed on this manner”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/27/3/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/DAG03.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV’s verses 23 and 24 are consecutive and the Aramaic quoted prints them consecutively (Aramaic 3:23, 3:24). Brenton’s Greek page prints a verse numbered 24, “and walked in the midst of the flame, singing praise to God”, and a verse numbered 25, “Then Azarias stood up, and prayed”, and continues to a verse labelled 90, and then prints the king’s astonishment at 91. Between the labels 23 and 91 the page prints sixty-five verses: 24 to 66, 71, 72, 72a, 72b and 73 to 90; the labels 67 to 70 are not on the page. None of these has a verse of the KJV’s numbering.",
    "scope": "T applies at Daniel 3:24. This part of Daniel is written in Aramaic, and the Aramaic is quoted from the same digital text; the record does not say what the Aramaic words quoted mean. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Daniel’s verse numbers differ between the KJV, the Hebrew and Aramaic text and Brenton’s Greek in places; the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s edition prints this Greek Daniel at the address cited under the title “Daniel (Greek)”. The same edition also prints a second English text titled Daniel, whose Greek page was not found at the address pattern used; the record does not say which Greek form of Daniel the page quoted is, and it does not compare any other Greek form. T applies at the point where the KJV continues from 3:23 to 3:24; the record quotes the first two verses of the Greek addition and the verse where the Greek returns to the king. The count of sixty-five is the count of verse labels printed on the cited page between 23 and 91 (checked by script), not a claim about any other Greek witness or edition. The record does not say what the added verses contain beyond the two quoted. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
