    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "At Joel 2:11 and 2:31 the KJV calls the day of the Lord “very terrible” and “terrible”. Brenton’s Greek prints ἐπιφανής and ἐπιφανῆ, which Brenton’s own English renders “very glorious” and “glorious”.",
    "text": "KJV Joel 2:11: “the day of the Lord is great and very terrible”. Hebrew (WLC) 2:11: גָדֹ֧ול יֹום־יְהוָ֛ה וְנֹורָ֥א מְאֹ֖ד. Brenton’s Greek 2:11: μεγάλη ἡ ἡμέρα Κυρίου, ἐπιφανὴς σφόδρα. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/JOL02.htm) prints: “the day of the Lord is great, very glorious”. KJV Joel 2:31: “the great and terrible day of the Lord”. Hebrew (WLC) 3:4: יֹ֣ום יְהוָ֔ה הַגָּדֹ֖ול וְהַנֹּורָֽא. Brenton’s Greek 3:4: τὴν ἡμέραν Κυρίου τὴν μεγάλην, καὶ ἐπιφανῆ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/JOL03.htm) prints: “the great and glorious day of the Lord”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/29/2/, https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/29/3/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOL02.htm and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/JOL03.htm.",
    "reasoning": "At 2:11 the Hebrew quoted prints גדול יום יהוה ונורא מאד, and at 2:31 יום יהוה הגדול והנורא. The Greek prints the same adjective, ἐπιφανής, at both places, after “great” (μεγάλη, μεγάλην). The KJV’s “terrible” and Brenton’s English “glorious” are not the same word and do not describe the day alike: one names what causes fear and the other names splendour.",
    "scope": "T applies at Joel 2:11 and Joel 2:31. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Joel’s verse numbers differ between the KJV and the Hebrew text from 2:28 (the KJV’s 2:28–32 are 3:1–5 in the Hebrew and in Brenton’s Greek, and the KJV’s chapter 3 is their chapter 4); the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
