    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Amos 1:3 charges Damascus with having “threshed Gilead with threshing instruments of iron”. Brenton’s Greek prints a verb that Brenton’s own English renders “sawed”, with “iron saws” and “the women with child of the Galaadites”.",
    "text": "KJV Amos 1:3: “because they have threshed Gilead with threshing instruments of iron”. Hebrew (WLC) 1:3: עַל־דּוּשָׁ֛ם בַּחֲרֻצֹ֥ות הַבַּרְזֶ֖ל אֶת־הַגִּלְעָֽד. Brenton’s Greek 1:3: ἀνθʼ ὧν ἔπριζον πριοσι σιδηροῖς τὰς ἐν γαστρὶ ἐχούσας τῶν ἐν Γαλαάδ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/AMO01.htm) prints: “because they sawed with iron saws the women with child of the Galaadites”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/30/1/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/AMO01.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints על דושם בחרצות הברזל את הגלעד. The KJV’s object is Gilead, and the act is threshing. The Greek words quoted print the women with child (τὰς ἐν γαστρὶ ἐχούσας) as the object, and the act is sawing. The object and the act differ.",
    "scope": "T applies at Amos 1:3. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The KJV also has women with child of Gilead at 1:13; the record does not say whether 1:3 and 1:13 describe one event. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
