    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "At Amos 1:6 and 1:9 the KJV has Gaza and Tyre deliver up “the whole captivity” to Edom. Brenton’s Greek prints τοῦ Σαλωμών, which Brenton’s own English renders “of Solomon”, after the noun for captivity at both verses.",
    "text": "KJV Amos 1:6: “they carried away captive the whole captivity”. Hebrew (WLC) 1:6: הַגְלֹותָ֛ם גָּל֥וּת שְׁלֵמָ֖ה. Brenton’s Greek 1:6: αἰχμαλωτεῦσαι αὐτοὺς αἰχμαλωσίαν τοῦ Σαλωμὼν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/AMO01.htm) prints: “they took prisoners the captivity of Solomon”. KJV Amos 1:9: “they delivered up the whole captivity to Edom”. Hebrew (WLC) 1:9: הַסְגִּירָ֞ם גָּל֤וּת שְׁלֵמָה֙ לֶאֱדֹ֔ום. Brenton’s Greek 1:9: συνέκλεισαν αἰχμαλωσίαν τοῦ Σαλωμὼν εἰς τὴν Ἰδουμαίαν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/AMO01.htm) prints: “they shut up the prisoners of Solomon into Idumea”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/30/1/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/AMO01.htm.",
    "reasoning": "At 1:6 the Hebrew quoted prints הגלותם גלות שלמה, and at 1:9 הסגירם גלות שלמה לאדום. The KJV renders the word after “captivity” as an adjective, “whole”. The Greek prints a man’s name, Solomon, in the genitive at both places. An adjective and a name are different words.",
    "scope": "T applies at Amos 1:6 and Amos 1:9. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The record does not say who the Solomon of the Greek is. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
