    "title": "“declareth unto man what is his thought” — the Greek has “proclaims to men his Christ”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Amos 4:13 has the Lord “declareth unto man what is his thought”. Brenton’s Greek prints τὸν χριστὸν αὐτοῦ, which Brenton’s own English renders “his Christ”. In the same verse the KJV’s “formeth the mountains” stands against a Greek phrase that Brenton’s own English renders “strengthens the thunder”.",
    "text": "KJV Amos 4:13: “declareth unto man what is his thought” … “he that formeth the mountains, and createth the wind”. Hebrew (WLC) 4:13: וּמַגִּ֤יד לְאָדָם֙ מַה־שֵּׂחֹ֔ו … יֹוצֵ֨ר הָרִ֜ים וּבֹרֵ֣א ר֗וּחַ. Brenton’s Greek 4:13: ἀπαγγέλλων εἰς ἀνθρώπους τὸν χριστὸν αὐτοῦ … ἐγὼ στερεῶν βροντὴν, καὶ κτίζων πνεῦμα. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/AMO04.htm) prints: “proclaims to men his Christ” … “I am he that strengthens the thunder, and creates the wind”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/30/4/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/AMO04.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints ומגיד לאדם מה שחו, and the KJV renders the last two words “what is his thought”. The Greek prints a noun with the article and a pronoun, τὸν χριστὸν αὐτοῦ, which Brenton’s own English renders “his Christ”, as the thing proclaimed to men. The KJV’s object is a thought; the Greek’s is a noun that Brenton’s own English renders “Christ”. A thought and a Christ are different things. In the first clause the Hebrew quoted prints יוצר הרים; the KJV has “formeth the mountains” and the Greek has στερεῶν βροντήν.",
