    "summary": "The KJV of Obadiah 1:20 has the captivity of Jerusalem “which is in Sepharad”. Brenton’s Greek prints ἕως Ἐφραθά, which Brenton’s own English renders “as far as Ephratha”, and no Sepharad.",
    "text": "KJV Obadiah 1:20: “the captivity of Jerusalem, which is in Sepharad”. Hebrew (WLC) 1:20: וְגָלֻ֥ת יְרוּשָׁלִַ֖ם אֲשֶׁ֣ר בִּסְפָרַ֑ד. Brenton’s Greek 1:20: καὶ ἡ μετοικεσία Ἱερουσαλὴμ ἕως Ἐφραθά. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/OBA01.htm) prints: “and the captives of Jerusalem shall inherit as far as Ephratha”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/31/1/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/OBA01.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints וגלת ירושלם אשר בספרד. The KJV prints the name Sepharad after “which is in”. The Greek prints a different name, Ephratha, after “as far as”. The two names differ, and the two phrases differ: a place where the captivity is, and a point to which the captives inherit.",
