    "summary": "The KJV of Habakkuk 1:5 begins “Behold ye among the heathen”. Brenton’s Greek prints οἱ καταφρονηταί, which Brenton’s own English renders “ye despisers”. The Greek of Acts 13:41, quoted in the KJV as “Behold, ye despisers”, prints the same word.",
    "text": "KJV Habakkuk 1:5: “Behold ye among the heathen”. Hebrew (WLC) 1:5: רְא֤וּ בַגֹּויִם֙. Brenton’s Greek 1:5: Ἴδετε οἱ καταφρονηταὶ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/HAB01.htm) prints: “Behold, ye despisers”. KJV Acts 13:41: “Behold, ye despisers”. Greek New Testament Acts 13:41 (Tischendorf, eighth edition): ἴδετε, οἱ καταφρονηταί. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/35/1/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/HAB01.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/44/13/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/44/13/.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints ראו בגוים. The KJV addresses the hearers as being among the nations, “the heathen”. The Greek addresses them with a noun for those who look down on something, “despisers”, and the Greek of Acts 13:41 prints the same noun. A place among the nations and a character as despisers are different descriptions of those addressed.",
    "scope": "T applies at Habakkuk 1:5. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The record does not examine how Acts 13 uses Habakkuk and does not say which wording Acts 13 follows beyond the words printed. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
