    "title": "“his soul which is lifted up is not upright in him: but the just shall live by his faith” — the Greek has “If he should draw back, my soul has no pleasure in him: but the just shall live by my faith”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Habakkuk 2:4 has “his soul which is lifted up is not upright in him” and “the just shall live by his faith”. Brenton’s Greek prints “If he should draw back, my soul has no pleasure in him” and “the just shall live by my faith”, in Brenton’s own English. The Greek of Hebrews 10:38 prints οὐκ εὐδοκεῖ ἡ ψυχή μου ἐν αὐτῷ, “my soul shall have no pleasure in him” in the KJV.",
    "text": "KJV Habakkuk 2:4: “his soul which is lifted up is not upright in him” … “but the just shall live by his faith”. Hebrew (WLC) 2:4: עֻפְּלָ֔ה לֹא־יָשְׁרָ֥ה נַפְשֹׁ֖ו בֹּ֑ו … וְצַדִּ֖יק בֶּאֱמוּנָתֹ֥ו. Brenton’s Greek 2:4: Ἐὰν ὑποστείληται, οὐκ εὐδοκεῖ ἡ ψυχή μου ἐν αὐτῷ … ἐκ πίστεώς μου ζήσεται. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/HAB02.htm) prints: “If he should draw back, my soul has no pleasure in him” … “live by my faith”. KJV Hebrews 10:38: “if any man draw back, my soul shall have no pleasure in him”. Greek New Testament Hebrews 10:38 (Tischendorf, eighth edition): οὐκ εὐδοκεῖ ἡ ψυχή μου ἐν αὐτῷ. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/35/2/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/HAB02.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/58/10/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/58/10/.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints עפלה לא ישרה נפשו בו and וצדיק באמונתו. In the first clause the KJV’s soul is the proud man’s own soul and is not upright; the Greek’s soul is the speaker’s, who has no pleasure in the one who draws back. In the second clause the KJV prints “his faith”, without independently settling the pronoun’s antecedent; the Greek prints μου, “my”, where the KJV prints “his”. Whose soul is spoken of, and whose faith, differs between the two.",
    "scope": "T applies at Habakkuk 2:4. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. T applies at the two clauses of this verse. The record does not examine how Romans 1:17, Galatians 3:11 or Hebrews 10:38 use the verse; the Greek of Hebrews 10:38 is given only to show that it prints the clause that Brenton’s Greek of Habakkuk prints. The Hebrew word that the KJV renders “faith” is not examined for its range of meaning. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
