    "title": "“he hath bid his guests” — who is the sacrifice and who are the guests?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "Zephaniah 1:7 has the Lord prepare “a sacrifice” and bid “his guests”, and 1:8 names “the day of the Lord's sacrifice”. The verses do not say what the sacrifice is or who the guests are.",
    "text": "KJV Zephaniah 1:7: “the Lord hath prepared a sacrifice, he hath bid his guests”. KJV Zephaniah 1:8: “in the day of the Lord's sacrifice, that I will punish the princes”.",
    "reasoning": "The sacrifice is prepared by the Lord and the guests are bidden by him, and the next verse describes a punishing of princes, the king’s children and those in strange apparel on “the day of the Lord's sacrifice”. The passage does not say what is sacrificed, whether the guests are those who will be judged or those who will carry it out, or whether the sacrifice is a figure for the judgment itself. Possible readings, named only as readings: the judged themselves as the sacrifice and a hostile nation as the guests; a sacrificial feast as a figure for the day of the Lord; a particular event not named in the verse. The record does not decide between them. Identifying the sacrifice and the guests matters to whether the imagery assigns them the roles of subjects of judgment or agents carrying it out; the record leaves those roles unresolved.",
    "scope": "Q marks a question the passage leaves open. It is not a finding of contradiction or of scientific error. The question is limited to the verses quoted. The record does not say that any reading is the right one, does not rest on the Hebrew or the Greek, and does not claim that no other passage says more.",
