    "title": "“Heldai, Tobijah, and Jedaiah” and “Helem, … Tobijah, … Jedaiah, … Hen” — the Greek has “the chief men” and “the useful men” and no such names",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Zechariah 6:10 and 6:14 names men of the captivity: “Heldai … Tobijah … Jedaiah” and “Helem … Tobijah … Jedaiah … Hen the son of Zephaniah”. Brenton’s Greek prints common words in those places, which Brenton’s own English renders “the chief men”, “the useful men”, “them that have understood it”, “them that wait patiently” and “the favour of the son of Sophonias”.",
    "text": "KJV Zechariah 6:10: “of Heldai, of Tobijah, and of Jedaiah”. Hebrew (WLC) 6:10: מֵחֶלְדַּ֕י וּמֵאֵ֥ת טֹובִיָּ֖ה וּמֵאֵ֣ת יְדַֽעְיָ֑ה. Brenton’s Greek 6:10: παρὰ τῶν ἀρχόντων, καὶ παρὰ τῶν χρησίμων αὐτῆς, καὶ παρὰ τῶν ἐπεγνωκότων αὐτὴν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ZEC06.htm) prints: “from the chief men, and from the useful men of it, and from them that have understood it”. KJV Zechariah 6:14: “to Helem, and to Tobijah, and to Jedaiah, and to Hen the son of Zephaniah”. Hebrew (WLC) 6:14: לְחֵ֙לֶם֙ וּלְטֹובִיָּ֣ה וְלִידַֽעְיָ֔ה וּלְחֵ֖ן בֶּן־צְפַנְיָ֑ה. Brenton’s Greek 6:14: τοῖς ὑπομένουσι, καὶ τοῖς χρησίμοις αὐτῆς, καὶ τοῖς ἐπεγνωκόσιν αὐτὴν, καὶ εἰς χάριτα υἱοῦ Σοφονίου. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ZEC06.htm) prints: “to them that wait patiently, and to the useful men of the captivity, and to them that have known it, and for the favour of the son of Sophonias”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/38/6/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ZEC06.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV prints personal names where the supplied Greek prints descriptions of groups and favour. The comparison concerns how the silver and gold contributors and the crown memorial are described, rather than establishing different people. Named individuals and the described groups may refer to the same people.",
    "scope": "T applies at Zechariah 6:10 and Zechariah 6:14. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The record does not say who the men were, and does not say that the Hebrew words are names; it says only that the KJV prints names where Brenton’s Greek prints common words. Zephaniah’s name appears in both texts at 6:14 in the form each edition prints. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The names and descriptions may identify the same people; different recipients are not established.",
