    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Zechariah 10:2 has the people troubled “because there was no shepherd”, after “the idols have spoken vanity”. Brenton’s Greek prints οὐκ ἦν ἴασις, which Brenton’s own English renders “there was no healing”, and οἱ ἀποφθεγγόμενοι, “the speakers”, for the idols.",
    "text": "KJV Zechariah 10:2: “the idols have spoken vanity” … “because there was no shepherd”. Hebrew (WLC) 10:2: הַתְּרָפִ֣ים דִּבְּרוּ־אָ֗וֶן … כִּֽי־אֵ֥ין רֹעֶֽה. Brenton’s Greek 10:2: οἱ ἀποφθεγγόμενοι ἐλάλησαν κόπους … διότι οὐκ ἦν ἴασις. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ZEC10.htm) prints: “the speakers have uttered grievous things” … “because there was no healing”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/38/10/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ZEC10.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints התרפים דברו און and כי אין רעה. At the end of the verse the KJV’s lack is a shepherd, the figure the flock image calls for. The Greek’s lack is healing. At the start the KJV’s speakers are idols and the Greek’s are “the speakers”, a different word for who spoke. A missing shepherd and a missing healing are different lacks.",
