    "title": "“O poor of the flock” and “the poor of the flock that waited upon me” — the Greek has “in the land of Chanaan” and “the Chananites”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Zechariah 11:7 and 11:11 has “the poor of the flock”. Brenton’s Greek prints εἰς τὴν Χαναανίτιν, which Brenton’s own English renders “in the land of Chanaan”, at 11:7 and οἱ Χαναναῖοι, “the Chananites”, at 11:11.",
    "text": "KJV Zechariah 11:7: “even you, O poor of the flock”. Hebrew (WLC) 11:7: לָכֵ֖ן עֲנִיֵּ֣י הַצֹּ֑אן. Brenton’s Greek 11:7: εἰς τὴν Χαναανίτιν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ZEC11.htm) prints: “in the land of Chanaan”. KJV Zechariah 11:11: “the poor of the flock that waited upon me”. Hebrew (WLC) 11:11: עֲנִיֵּ֤י הַצֹּאן֙ הַשֹּׁמְרִ֣ים אֹתִ֔י. Brenton’s Greek 11:11: οἱ Χαναναῖοι τὰ πρόβατα τὰ φυλασσόμενά μοι. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ZEC11.htm) prints: “the Chananites, the sheep that are kept for me”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/38/11/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ZEC11.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints לכן עניי הצאן at 11:7 and עניי הצאן השמרים אתי at 11:11. The KJV’s phrase names the afflicted of the flock. The Greek names a land and a people, Canaan and the Canaanites. The KJV describes people by their condition as the poor of the flock; the Greek names a land at 11:7 and a people at 11:11. The descriptions differ, but may concern the same people.",
    "scope": "T applies at Zechariah 11:7 and Zechariah 11:11. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The record does not say that the Hebrew phrase can or cannot be read as a name. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. This does not establish different persons or exclude poor people among the named inhabitants.",
