    "title": "“Cast it unto the potter” — the Greek has “Drop them into the furnace”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Zechariah 11:13 has the Lord say “Cast it unto the potter”, and the prophet cast the silver “to the potter in the house of the Lord”. Brenton’s Greek prints εἰς τὸ χωνευτήριον, which Brenton’s own English renders “into the furnace”, at both places. The Greek of Matthew 27:10 prints τοῦ κεραμέως, “the potter’s”, in “the potter’s field”.",
    "text": "KJV Zechariah 11:13: “Cast it unto the potter” … “cast them to the potter in the house of the Lord”. Hebrew (WLC) 11:13: הַשְׁלִיכֵ֙הוּ֙ אֶל־הַיֹּוצֵ֔ר … וָאַשְׁלִ֥יךְ אֹתֹ֛ו בֵּ֥ית יְהוָ֖ה אֶל־הַיֹּוצֵֽר. Brenton’s Greek 11:13: κάθες αὐτοὺς εἰς τὸ χωνευτήριον … ἐνέβαλον αὐτοὺς εἰς τὸν οἶκον Κυρίου εἰς τὸ χωνευτήριον. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ZEC11.htm) prints: “Drop them into the furnace” … “cast them into the furnace in the house of the Lord”. KJV Matthew 27:10: “gave them for the potter's field”. Greek New Testament Matthew 27:10 (Tischendorf, eighth edition): εἰς τὸν ἀγρὸν τοῦ κεραμέως. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/38/11/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ZEC11.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/27/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/27/.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints השליכהו אל היוצר and ואשליך אתו בית יהוה אל היוצר. The KJV’s recipient is a potter, a person. The Greek’s is a furnace, χωνευτήριον, a place or vessel for melting metal. A potter and a furnace are different recipients of the thirty pieces of silver. The Greek of Matthew 27:10 prints a word for a potter.",
    "scope": "T applies at Zechariah 11:13. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The record does not examine how Matthew 27:9–10 uses the verse; that comparison is in a separate record. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
