    "text": "KJV Zechariah 13:7: “against my shepherd, and against the man that is my fellow” … “smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered”. Hebrew (WLC) 13:7: עַל־רֹעִי֙ וְעַל־גֶּ֣בֶר עֲמִיתִ֔י … הַ֤ךְ אֶת־הָֽרֹעֶה֙ וּתְפוּצֶ֣יןָ הַצֹּ֔אן. Brenton’s Greek 13:7: ἐπὶ τοὺς ποιμένας μου, καὶ ἐπὶ ἄνδρα πολίτην μου … πατάξατε τοὺς ποιμένας, καὶ ἐκσπάσατε τὰ πρόβατα. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ZEC13.htm) prints: “against my shepherds, and against the man who is my citizen” … “smite the shepherds, and draw out the sheep”. KJV Matthew 26:31: “I will smite the shepherd, and the sheep of the flock shall be scattered abroad”. Greek New Testament Matthew 26:31 (Tischendorf, eighth edition): πατάξω τὸν ποιμένα. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/38/13/, https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ZEC13.htm, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/26/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/26/.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints על רעי ועל גבר עמיתי and הך את הרעה ותפוצין הצאן. The KJV has one shepherd, and the sheep are scattered as a result of the smiting. The Greek has several shepherds who are to be smitten, and the sheep are drawn out. One shepherd and many, and scattering and drawing out, differ.",
