    "summary": "The KJV of Zechariah 14:5 has “ye shall flee to the valley of the mountains”. Brenton’s Greek prints a verb that Brenton’s own English renders “shall be closed up” and a different name at the end, “Jasod”, where the KJV has Azal.",
    "text": "KJV Zechariah 14:5: “ye shall flee to the valley of the mountains; for the valley of the mountains shall reach unto Azal”. Hebrew (WLC) 14:5: וְנַסְתֶּ֣ם גֵּֽיא־הָרַ֗י כִּֽי־יַגִּ֣יעַ גֵּי־הָרִים֮ אֶל־אָצַל֒. Brenton’s Greek 14:5: Καὶ φραχθήσεται ἡ φάραγξ τῶν ὀρέων μου, καὶ ἐγκολληθήσεται φάραγξ ὀρέων ἕως Ἰασὸδ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/ZEC14.htm) prints: “And the valley of my mountains shall be closed up, and the valley of the mountains shall be joined on to Jasod”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/38/14/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/ZEC14.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints ונסתם גיא הרי כי יגיע גי הרים אל אצל. The KJV’s verb is “flee” and the valley is a refuge to which they flee. The Greek’s verb is a passive for being stopped up or blocked, and the valley is the one that shall be closed. Fleeing to a valley and a valley shut up are different things. The name forms alone do not establish a meaning difference or distinct places.",
