    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Matthew 27:9 says “that which was spoken by Jeremy the prophet” and gives “And they took the thirty pieces of silver”. The KJV has “thirty pieces of silver” and the casting to the potter in Zechariah 11:12–13, and the packaged KJV of Jeremiah does not print the phrase.",
    "text": "KJV Matthew 27:9: “Then was fulfilled that which was spoken by Jeremy the prophet, saying, And they took the thirty pieces of silver”. Greek New Testament Matthew 27:9 (Tischendorf, eighth edition): διὰ Ἰερεμίου τοῦ προφήτου. KJV Matthew 27:10: “And gave them for the potter's field”. KJV Zechariah 11:12: “So they weighed for my price thirty pieces of silver”. Hebrew (WLC) 11:12: שְׁלֹשִׁ֥ים כָּֽסֶף. KJV Zechariah 11:13: “I took the thirty pieces of silver, and cast them to the potter in the house of the Lord”. Hebrew (WLC) 11:13: שְׁלֹשִׁ֣ים הַכֶּ֔סֶף. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/38/11/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/27/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/27/.",
    "reasoning": "Matthew names the prophet Jeremiah (Ἰερεμίου) as the one who spoke the words, “they took the thirty pieces of silver”. The same sum, thirty pieces of silver, and the casting of it to the potter in the house of the Lord are printed in Zechariah 11:12–13. A search of the packaged KJV for “thirty pieces of silver” finds it in Zechariah 11:12 and 11:13 and in Matthew, and not in Jeremiah. The verses could both stand: Matthew may be naming the prophetic book under whose name the passage is placed, or combining Zechariah with Jeremiah’s verses about a potter (chapters 18 and 19) and a field bought (chapter 32); the verse does not say which. The record does not choose between these.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the record compares the KJV names and wording of the passages and does not rest on a difference between the KJV and the Hebrew or Greek. It does not say that Matthew is mistaken, does not say that Jeremiah’s book contains or lacks any other words of the quotation, and does not examine other manuscripts of Matthew 27:9 or any reading that names another prophet. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text; the Greek of Matthew is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life, with the Textus Receptus there carrying the same name. Neither is a critical apparatus. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
