    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Malachi 2:16 has the Lord say “that he hateth putting away”. Brenton’s Greek prints ἐὰν μισήσας ἐξαποστείλῃς, which Brenton’s own English renders “if thou shouldest hate thy wife and put her away”, with “saith the Lord” after it.",
    "text": "KJV Malachi 2:16: “the Lord, the God of Israel, saith that he hateth putting away”. Hebrew (WLC) 2:16: כִּֽי־שָׂנֵ֣א שַׁלַּ֗ח אָמַ֤ר יְהוָה֙ אֱלֹהֵ֣י יִשְׂרָאֵ֔ל. Brenton’s Greek 2:16: Ἀλλὰ ἐὰν μισήσας ἐξαποστείλῃς, λέγει Κύριος ὁ Θεὸς τοῦ Ἰσραὴλ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/MAL02.htm) prints: “if thou shouldest hate thy wife and put her away, saith the Lord God of Israel”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/39/2/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/MAL02.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints כי שנא שלח אמר יהוה אלהי ישראל. In the KJV the Lord is the one who hates, and what he hates is putting away. In the Greek the one who hates is the person addressed, and the Greek has a condition: if he hates and sends away. The KJV states what the Lord hates; the Greek states what a man does, and what follows.",
    "scope": "T applies at Malachi 2:16. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. Brenton’s own English supplies “thy wife”; the Greek words quoted do not print a noun for the wife. The record does not say which reading is original. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
