    "title": "“Will a man rob God?” and “Ye are cursed with a curse” — the Greek has “Will a man insult God?” and “ye do surely look off from me”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Malachi 3:8 asks “Will a man rob God?” and 3:9 says “Ye are cursed with a curse”. Brenton’s Greek prints a verb that Brenton’s own English renders “insult” at 3:8, and ἀποβλέποντες ὑμεῖς ἀποβλέπετε, “ye do surely look off from me”, at 3:9.",
    "text": "KJV Malachi 3:8: “Will a man rob God?”. Hebrew (WLC) 3:8: הֲיִקְבַּ֨ע אָדָ֜ם אֱלֹהִ֗ים. Brenton’s Greek 3:8: Μήτι πτερνιεῖ ἄνθρωπος Θεόν. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/MAL03.htm) prints: “Will a man insult God”. KJV Malachi 3:9: “Ye are cursed with a curse”. Hebrew (WLC) 3:9: בַּמְּאֵרָה֙ אַתֶּ֣ם נֵֽאָרִ֔ים. Brenton’s Greek 3:9: Καὶ ἀποβλέποντες ὑμεῖς ἀποβλέπετε. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/MAL03.htm) prints: “And ye do surely look off from me”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/39/3/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/MAL03.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints היקבע אדם אלהים at 3:8 and במארה אתם נארים at 3:9. The KJV’s charge is robbery, and its pronouncement is a curse on the people. The Greek’s charge is a verb that Brenton’s own English renders “insult”, and at 3:9 it prints a phrase about looking away from the Lord, with no curse. Robbing and insulting, and a curse and looking away, are different charges and different pronouncements.",
    "scope": "T applies at Malachi 3:8 and Malachi 3:9. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. T applies at the clauses quoted in the two verses; the record does not examine the Hebrew verb for its other uses and does not say which Greek or English word is the better rendering. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
