    "title": "“Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse” — the Greek has “The year is completed, and ye have brought all the produce into the storehouses”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Malachi 3:10 opens with a command, “Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse, that there may be meat in mine house”. Brenton’s Greek prints a statement that Brenton’s own English renders “The year is completed, and ye have brought all the produce into the storehouses; but there shall be the plunder thereof in its house”.",
    "text": "KJV Malachi 3:10: “Bring ye all the tithes into the storehouse, that there may be meat in mine house”. Hebrew (WLC) 3:10: הָבִ֨יאוּ אֶת־כָּל־הַֽמַּעֲשֵׂ֜ר אֶל־בֵּ֣ית הָאֹוצָ֗ר וִיהִ֥י טֶ֙רֶף֙ בְּבֵיתִ֔י. Brenton’s Greek 3:10: Τὸ ἔτος συνετελέσθη, καὶ εἰσηνέγκατε πάντα τὰ ἐκφόρια εἰς τοὺς θησαυροὺς, καὶ ἔσται ἡ διαρπαγὴ αὐτοῦ ἐν τῷ οἴκῳ αὐτοῦ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/MAL03.htm) prints: “The year is completed, and ye have brought all the produce into the storehouses; but there shall be the plunder thereof in its house”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/39/3/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/MAL03.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints הביאו את כל המעשר אל בית האוצר ויהי טרף בביתי. The KJV’s opening is an imperative, a call to bring the tithes so that there will be food in the Lord’s house. The Greek’s opening says that the year is over and the produce has been brought in, and then says that the plunder of it will be in its house. A command to bring and a statement that it has been brought are different.",
    "scope": "T applies at Malachi 3:10. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry.",
