    "title": "“the proud” — the Greek has “strangers” and “aliens” (Malachi 3:15 and 4:1)",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Malachi 3:15 has “we call the proud happy” and 4:1 has “all the proud … shall be stubble”. Brenton’s Greek prints ἀλλοτρίους, which Brenton’s own English renders “strangers”, at 3:15, and οἱ ἀλλογενεῖς, “the aliens”, at 4:1.",
    "text": "KJV Malachi 3:15: “we call the proud happy”. Hebrew (WLC) 3:15: מְאַשְּׁרִ֣ים זֵדִ֑ים. Brenton’s Greek 3:15: μακαρίζομεν ἀλλοτρίους. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/MAL03.htm) prints: “we pronounce strangers blessed”. KJV Malachi 4:1: “all the proud, yea, and all that do wickedly, shall be stubble”. Hebrew (WLC) 3:19: כָל־זֵדִ֜ים וְכָל־עֹשֵׂ֤ה רִשְׁעָה֙ קַ֔שׁ. Brenton’s Greek 3:19: πάντες οἱ ἀλλογενεῖς, καὶ πάντες οἱ ποιοῦντες ἄνομα, καλάμη. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/MAL03.htm) prints: “all the aliens, and all that do wickedly, shall be stubble”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/39/3/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/MAL03.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints מאשרים זדים at 3:15 and כל זדים at 4:1, the same word twice. The KJV renders it “the proud” both times, a word for those who are arrogant. The Greek prints a word for those who belong to another family or people, “strangers” at 3:15 and “aliens” at 4:1. Arrogance and belonging to another people are different descriptions; the wording does not establish that the groups cannot overlap.",
    "scope": "T applies at Malachi 3:15 and Malachi 4:1. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Malachi’s verse numbers differ between the KJV and the Hebrew text in chapter 4 (the KJV’s 4:n is 3:(n+18) in the Hebrew, and Brenton’s Greek prints the KJV’s 4:5 and 4:6 as its 3:22 and 3:23 and the KJV’s 4:4 as its 3:24); the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The comparison does not identify the people or establish disjoint groups.",
