    "title": "“Elijah the prophet” and “the great and dreadful day of the Lord” — the Greek has “Elias the Thesbite” and “the great and glorious day of the Lord”",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Malachi 4:5 has “Elijah the prophet” and “the great and dreadful day of the Lord”. Brenton’s Greek prints Ἠλίαν τὸν Θεσβίτην, “Elias the Thesbite” in Brenton’s own English, and τὴν μεγάλην καὶ ἐπιφανῆ, “the great and glorious”.",
    "text": "KJV Malachi 4:5: “Elijah the prophet” … “the great and dreadful day of the Lord”. Hebrew (WLC) 3:23: אֵלִיָּ֣ה הַנָּבִ֑יא … הַגָּדֹ֖ול וְהַנֹּורָֽא. Brenton’s Greek 3:22: Ἠλίαν τὸν Θεσβίτην … τὴν ἡμέραν Κυρίου τὴν μεγάλην καὶ ἐπιφανῆ. Brenton’s own English (https://ebible.org/eng-Brenton/MAL03.htm) prints: “Elias the Thesbite” … “the great and glorious day of the Lord”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/WLC/39/3/ and https://ebible.org/grcbrent/MAL03.htm.",
    "reasoning": "The Hebrew quoted prints אליה הנביא and הגדול והנורא. The KJV’s title for Elijah is “the prophet” and its adjective for the day is “dreadful”. The Greek’s title is “the Thesbite”, a man of Tishbe, and its adjective is one that Brenton’s own English renders “glorious”. A prophet and a Thesbite are different descriptions of the man, and a dreadful day and a glorious day are different descriptions of the day.",
    "scope": "T applies at Malachi 4:5. The record does not say what the Hebrew words quoted mean, and does not say which Greek word corresponds to which Hebrew word. This compares the wording of the texts quoted. It does not show which wording is original. Malachi’s verse numbers differ between the KJV and the Hebrew text in chapter 4 (the KJV’s 4:n is 3:(n+18) in the Hebrew, and Brenton’s Greek prints the KJV’s 4:5 and 4:6 as its 3:22 and 3:23 and the KJV’s 4:4 as its 3:24); the numbers quoted in the text lines are the numbers of the text quoted, and the refs use the KJV numbering. The English quoted from Brenton’s own page is given only to show how that edition renders the Greek it prints; it is not a lexicon entry. The Hebrew quoted is the Westminster Leningrad Codex digital text. A digital named edition is not proof that every manuscript agrees; other Hebrew manuscripts were not examined. The Greek quoted is Brenton’s printed edition of the Septuagint, an ancient Greek translation tradition of the Old Testament. It is not a critical apparatus and does not show that all Greek witnesses agree or which reading is original. English in square brackets is the researcher’s plain rendering, not a KJV quotation and not a lexicon entry. The titles may describe the same Elijah, and glorious does not by itself exclude a fearful day. The qualifying comparison includes the explicitly different prophet and Thesbite descriptions; no different person or incompatible day is established.",
