    "summary": "The KJV of Matthew 11:23 says Capernaum “art exalted unto heaven”. The Textus Receptus text served prints a participle, “having been exalted”; Tischendorf’s text prints μὴ … ὑψωθήσῃ, which opens a question.",
    "text": "KJV Matthew 11:23: “which art exalted unto heaven, shalt be brought down to hell”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 11:23: μὴ ἕως οὐρανοῦ ὑψωθήσῃ [shall you be exalted as far as heaven?]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Matthew 11:23: η εως του ουρανου υψωθεισα [you who have been exalted as far as heaven]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/11/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/11/.",
    "reasoning": "The KJV states, as a fact about the city, that it has been exalted to heaven. Tischendorf’s clause asks whether it will be exalted as far as heaven, with a negative particle that expects the answer no. A statement that the city is exalted and a question about whether it will be are different statements.",
    "scope": "T applies at Matthew 11:23. The difference is between Greek editions: the Textus Receptus text served agrees with the KJV and Tischendorf’s text does not. This record does not say which reading is original, and two digital editions are not a survey of manuscripts. The KJV’s “hell” at this verse is the subject of a separate record on the Greek words behind it. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
