    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Matthew 19:9 ends with a second statement, “and whoso marrieth her which is put away doth commit adultery”. The Textus Receptus text served prints it; Tischendorf’s text ends the verse after “commits adultery” in the first statement.",
    "text": "KJV Matthew 19:9: “except it be for fornication” … “and whoso marrieth her which is put away doth commit adultery”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 19:9: ὃς ἂν ἀπολύσῃ τὴν γυναῖκα αὐτοῦ μὴ ἐπὶ πορνείᾳ καὶ γαμήσῃ ἄλλην, μοιχᾶται [whoever divorces his wife, not for fornication, and marries another, commits adultery — the verse ends here]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Matthew 19:9: ει μη επι πορνεια και γαμηση αλλην μοιχαται και ο απολελυμενην γαμησας μοιχαται [unless for fornication, and marries another, commits adultery; and the one who marries her who has been divorced commits adultery]. KJV Matthew 5:32: “saving for the cause of fornication”. KJV Mark 10:11: “Whosoever shall put away his wife, and marry another, committeth adultery against her.”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/19/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/19/.",
    "reasoning": "Two things differ in the verse. The second statement, about the man who marries the divorced woman, is in one served edition only. And the exception clause has the particle εἰ before μὴ ἐπὶ πορνείᾳ in the Textus Receptus text served, where Tischendorf’s text has μὴ ἐπὶ πορνείᾳ alone. The KJV’s “except it be for fornication” follows the longer form.",
    "scope": "T applies at Matthew 19:9. The difference is between Greek editions: the Textus Receptus text served agrees with the KJV and Tischendorf’s text does not. This record does not say which reading is original, and two digital editions are not a survey of manuscripts. The record does not decide how μὴ ἐπὶ πορνείᾳ is read, does not discuss the exception clause’s place in 5:32 or Mark 10:11, and does not say which edition reflects the earlier text. A separate published record covers the Matthew 19:9 and Mark 10:11 divorce passages; this record concerns only the wording the two Greek editions print. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
