    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Matthew 19:17 has Jesus answer “Why callest thou me good? there is none good but one, that is, God”. The Textus Receptus text served prints that; Tischendorf’s text has “Why do you ask me about the good? There is one who is good”.",
    "text": "KJV Matthew 19:17: “Why callest thou me good? there is none good but one, that is, God”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 19:17: τί με ἐρωτᾷς περὶ τοῦ ἀγαθοῦ; εἷς ἐστιν ὁ ἀγαθός [Why do you ask me about the good? There is one who is good]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Matthew 19:17: τι με λεγεις αγαθον ουδεις αγαθος ει μη εις ο θεος [Why do you call me good? No one is good except one, God]. KJV Mark 10:18: “Why callest thou me good? there is none good but one, that is, God.”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/19/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/19/.",
    "reasoning": "In the KJV the question is about the title “good” given to Jesus and the answer names God as the only one who is good. In Tischendorf’s text the question is about “the good”, the thing asked about at 19:16, and the answer says there is one who is good without the phrase “that is, God”. The KJV of Mark 10:18 and Luke 18:19 print the KJV form of Matthew’s question.",
