    "text": "KJV Matthew 27:34: “They gave him vinegar to drink mingled with gall”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 27:34: ἔδωκαν αὐτῷ πεῖν οἶνον μετὰ χολῆς μεμιγμένον [they gave him wine to drink mixed with gall]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Matthew 27:34: εδωκαν αυτω πιειν οξος μετα χολης μεμιγμενον [they gave him sour wine to drink mixed with gall]. KJV Mark 15:23: “they gave him to drink wine mingled with myrrh”. KJV Psalms 69:21: “They gave me also gall for my meat; and in my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/27/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/27/.",
    "reasoning": "Vinegar and wine are named as different drinks. The KJV of Psalms 69:21 has the pair “gall” and “vinegar”, which the KJV of Matthew 27:34 follows; the KJV of Mark 15:23 has “wine mingled with myrrh”. Tischendorf’s text puts wine in Matthew’s verse.",
