    "title": "“hell” — the Greek prints two different words, Gehenna and Hades",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV prints “hell” at Matthew 5:22 for εἰς τὴν γέενναν and at 11:23 and 16:18 for ᾅδου. Both served Greek editions print γέεννα at 5:22 and ᾅδης at 11:23 and 16:18, two different words.",
    "text": "KJV Matthew 5:22: “in danger of hell fire”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 5:22: εἰς τὴν γέενναν τοῦ πυρός [into the Gehenna of fire]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Matthew 5:22: εις την γεενναν του πυρος [into the Gehenna of fire]. KJV Matthew 11:23: “shalt be brought down to hell”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 11:23: ἕως ᾅδου καταβιβασθήσῃ [as far as Hades you shall be brought down]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Matthew 11:23: εως αδου καταβιβασθηση [as far as Hades you shall be brought down]. KJV Matthew 16:18: “the gates of hell shall not prevail against it”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 16:18: πύλαι ᾅδου [gates of Hades]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Matthew 16:18: πυλαι αδου [gates of Hades]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/5/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/5/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/11/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/11/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/16/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/16/.",
    "reasoning": "The English is the same word; the Greek is two. At 5:22 the Greek has “the Gehenna of fire”. At 11:23 and 16:18 it has ᾅδης, in a clause about being brought down to it and in a clause about its gates. The English reader of the KJV cannot see from the English that two different Greek words stand behind “hell”.",
    "scope": "T applies at Matthew 5:22, Matthew 11:23 and Matthew 16:18. The Textus Receptus text and Tischendorf’s text served print the same Greek in the places quoted; the difference is between that Greek and the English the KJV prints for it. This record does not say what the Greek or the English means beyond the words quoted, and two digital editions are not a survey of manuscripts. The record does not say what Gehenna or Hades denotes, and does not say the KJV’s “hell” is wrong; it records only that one English word renders two different Greek words in the verses quoted. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
