    "summary": "The KJV prints “the end of the world” at Matthew 13:39, 24:3 and 28:20. Both served Greek editions print αἰῶνος, a word for an age, and συντέλεια, a completion, where the same KJV prints “the world” at 13:38 for κόσμος.",
    "text": "KJV Matthew 13:39: “the harvest is the end of the world”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 13:39: αἰῶνός ἐστιν [of the age it is]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Matthew 13:39: αιωνος εστιν [of the age it is]. KJV Matthew 24:3: “and of the end of the world?”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 24:3: συντελείας τοῦ αἰῶνος [of the completion of the age]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Matthew 24:3: της συντελειας του αιωνος [of the completion of the age]. KJV Matthew 28:20: “even unto the end of the world”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 28:20: τῆς συντελείας τοῦ αἰῶνος [of the completion of the age]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Matthew 28:20: της συντελειας του αιωνος [of the completion of the age]. KJV Matthew 13:38: “The field is the world”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 13:38: ἀγρός ἐστιν ὁ κόσμος. The Textus Receptus text served prints the same words. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/13/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/13/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/24/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/24/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/28/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/28/.",
    "reasoning": "Matthew 13:38 and 13:39 stand side by side: the field is “the world” (ὁ κόσμος) and the harvest is “the end of the world” (συντέλεια αἰῶνος). The KJV’s English uses “world” for both, but the Greek has two different nouns, one for the world and one for an age.",
    "scope": "T applies at Matthew 13:39, Matthew 24:3 and Matthew 28:20. The Textus Receptus text and Tischendorf’s text served print the same Greek in the places quoted; the difference is between that Greek and the English the KJV prints for it. This record does not say what the Greek or the English means beyond the words quoted, and two digital editions are not a survey of manuscripts. The record does not say that the two Greek nouns cannot be rendered by one English word, and does not examine other verses that print αἰών. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
