    "summary": "The KJV prints “farthing” at Matthew 5:26 for κοδράντης and at 10:29 for ἀσσάριον, two different words, in both served Greek editions.",
    "text": "KJV Matthew 5:26: “till thou hast paid the uttermost farthing”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 5:26: τὸν ἔσχατον κοδράντην [the last kodrantes]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Matthew 5:26: τον εσχατον κοδραντην [the last kodrantes]. KJV Matthew 10:29: “Are not two sparrows sold for a farthing?”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 10:29: δύο στρουθία ἀσσαρίου πωλεῖται [two sparrows are sold for an assarion]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Matthew 10:29: δυο στρουθια ασσαριου πωλειται [two sparrows are sold for an assarion]. KJV Mark 12:42: “two mites, which make a farthing”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/5/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/5/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/10/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/10/.",
    "reasoning": "One English coin name renders two Greek coin names. Both are named as separate words in the Greek, and in the KJV’s Mark 12:42 “farthing” is again used for a small sum. A reader of the KJV cannot see that Matthew prints a different coin name at 5:26 and 10:29.",
    "scope": "T applies at Matthew 5:26 and Matthew 10:29. The Textus Receptus text and Tischendorf’s text served print the same Greek in the places quoted; the difference is between that Greek and the English the KJV prints for it. This record does not say what the Greek or the English means beyond the words quoted, and two digital editions are not a survey of manuscripts. The record does not state the values of either coin and does not say how the KJV’s “farthing” relates to either. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
