    "text": "KJV Mark 1:2: “As it is written in the prophets, Behold, I send my messenger”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Mark 1:2: καθὼς γέγραπται ἐν τῷ Ἡσαΐᾳ τῷ προφήτῃ [as it is written in Isaiah the prophet]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Mark 1:2: ως γεγραπται εν τοις προφηταις [as it is written in the prophets]. KJV Malachi 3:1: “Behold, I will send my messenger”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/41/1/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/41/1/.",
    "reasoning": "The first clause of the quotation, “Behold, I send my messenger”, is worded like the KJV of Malachi 3:1, “Behold, I will send my messenger”, and the verse that follows in Mark (1:3) is a different saying, about a voice crying in the wilderness. One edition attributes the written words to “the prophets” without a name; the other names Isaiah.",
    "scope": "T applies at Mark 1:2. The difference is between Greek editions: the Textus Receptus text served agrees with the KJV and Tischendorf’s text does not. This record does not say which reading is original, and two digital editions are not a survey of manuscripts. The record does not examine Isaiah 40:3 or the Greek of Malachi 3:1, and does not say which prophet the first clause comes from. Side B shows the KJV words of Malachi 3:1 that the first clause resembles. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
