    "title": "“purging all meats” — the participle differs in form between the editions",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "In the KJV of Mark 7:19 the food “goeth out into the draught, purging all meats”. The Textus Receptus text served prints the participle καθαρίζον, neuter, which agrees with the draught; Tischendorf’s text prints καθαρίζων, masculine, which does not.",
    "text": "KJV Mark 7:19: “goeth out into the draught, purging all meats”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Mark 7:19: καθαρίζων πάντα τὰ βρώματα [καθαρίζων, masculine singular, all the foods]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Mark 7:19: καθαριζον παντα τα βρωματα [καθαρίζον, neuter singular, all the foods]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/41/7/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/41/7/.",
    "reasoning": "The two editions print the same word with a different ending. In the neuter form the thing doing the purging is the draught, which is what the KJV has. In the masculine form the participle cannot belong to the draught or to the food, and the clause is then about someone else, with the same words “all the foods”. The KJV’s clause follows the first.",
