    "summary": "The KJV has Jesus go to “the borders of Tyre and Sidon” (Mark 7:24) and return “departing from the coasts of Tyre and Sidon” (7:31). The Textus Receptus text served prints both names at both verses. Tischendorf’s text has only Tyre at 7:24 and at 7:31 has him come “through Sidon” to the sea of Galilee.",
    "text": "KJV Mark 7:24: “the borders of Tyre and Sidon”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Mark 7:24: ἀπῆλθεν εἰς τὰ ὅρια Τύρου [went away into the region of Tyre]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Mark 7:24: απηλθεν εις τα μεθορια τυρου και σιδωνος [went away into the borders of Tyre and Sidon]. KJV Mark 7:31: “departing from the coasts of Tyre and Sidon, he came unto the sea of Galilee”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Mark 7:31: ἐκ τῶν ὁρίων Τύρου ἦλθεν διὰ Σιδῶνος εἰς τὴν θάλασσαν τῆς Γαλιλαίας [from the region of Tyre he came through Sidon to the sea of Galilee]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Mark 7:31: εκ των οριων τυρου και σιδωνος ηλθεν προς την θαλασσαν της γαλιλαιας [from the borders of Tyre and Sidon he came to the sea of Galilee]. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/41/7/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/41/7/.",
    "reasoning": "At 7:24 one edition names Tyre and Sidon and the other names Tyre alone. At 7:31 the same pair of cities is the place he left in one edition, and in the other Sidon is a place he passed through on the way to the sea. The two editions therefore give a different itinerary.",
