    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Mark 9:31 and 10:34 says the Son of man “shall rise the third day”. The Textus Receptus text served prints τῇ τρίτῃ ἡμέρᾳ, “on the third day”; Tischendorf’s text prints μετὰ τρεῖς ἡμέρας, “after three days”, at both verses.",
    "text": "KJV Mark 9:31: “he shall rise the third day”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Mark 9:31: μετὰ τρεῖς ἡμέρας ἀναστήσεται [after three days he will rise]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Mark 9:31: τη τριτη ημερα αναστησεται [on the third day he will rise]. KJV Mark 10:34: “the third day he shall rise again”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Mark 10:34: μετὰ τρεῖς ἡμέρας ἀναστήσεται [after three days he will rise]. Greek New Testament (Textus Receptus as served, printed there without accents) Mark 10:34: τη τριτη ημερα αναστησεται [on the third day he will rise]. KJV Mark 8:31: “after three days rise again”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Mark 8:31: μετὰ τρεῖς ἡμέρας ἀναστῆναι. The Textus Receptus text served prints the same words. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/41/9/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/41/9/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/41/10/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/41/10/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/41/8/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/41/8/.",
    "reasoning": "The two phrases are different statements of the time: “on the third day” and “after three days”. In the KJV, Mark 8:31 already has “after three days”, and both served editions agree there, so the KJV of Mark carries both phrases. In Tischendorf’s text all three predictions have “after three days”.",
