    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of John 9:39 has Jesus say “For judgment I am come into this world, that they which see not might see; and that they which see might be made blind”. The KJV of John 12:47 has him say “I came not to judge the world, but to save the world”, and 3:17 says God “sent not his Son into the world to condemn the world”.",
    "text": "KJV John 9:39: “For judgment I am come into this world”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) John 9:39: εἰς κρίμα ἐγὼ εἰς τὸν κόσμον τοῦτον ἦλθον. KJV John 12:47: “I came not to judge the world”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) John 12:47: οὐ γὰρ ἦλθον ἵνα κρίνω τὸν κόσμον. KJV John 3:17: “to condemn the world”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) John 3:17: ἵνα κρίνῃ τὸν κόσμον. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/43/9/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/43/9/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/43/12/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/43/12/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/43/3/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/43/3/.",
    "reasoning": "Both verses say why he came into the world, and one gives judgment as the purpose while the other says he did not come to judge. The Greek words are different: εἰς κρίμα (for a judgment, a decision) in 9:39, and ἵνα κρίνω (to judge) in 12:47. They can stand together if the first is the outcome of his coming and the second its aim, which 9:39 may suggest by its “that”; neither verse says so.",
