    "id": "they-held-him-by-the-feet-or-touch-me-not-matthew-28-9-john-20-17",
    "mode": "contradictions",
    "ref": "Matthew 28:9",
    "appliesTo": [
      "Matthew 28:9",
      "John 20:17"
    ],
    "title": "The women “held him by the feet” (Matthew 28:9) or “Touch me not; for I am not yet ascended” (John 20:17)?",
    "status": "Context",
    "summary": "The KJV of Matthew 28:9 has the women, when Jesus meets them, take hold of him: “they came and held him by the feet, and worshipped him”. The KJV of John 20:17 has Jesus say to Mary Magdalene “Touch me not; for I am not yet ascended to my Father”, and Luke 24:39 has him say to the eleven “handle me, and see”.",
    "text": "KJV Matthew 28:9: “they came and held him by the feet”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Matthew 28:9: αἱ δὲ προσελθοῦσαι ἐκράτησαν αὐτοῦ τοὺς πόδας. KJV John 20:17: “Touch me not; for I am not yet ascended to my Father”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) John 20:17: μή μου ἅπτου· οὔπω γὰρ ἀναβέβηκα. KJV Luke 24:39: “handle me, and see”. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/40/28/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/40/28/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/43/20/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/43/20/.",
    "reasoning": "One account has Jesus allow himself to be held by the feet and the other has him tell Mary not to touch him. The accounts may be of different meetings, since Matthew has more than one woman and John has Mary alone, and the reason in John 20:17 is stated as “for I am not yet ascended”, which a later meeting would not share; neither passage says whether they are one.",
    "scope": "No tag is proposed: the record compares the wording of the passages and does not rest on a difference between the KJV and the Greek. It does not say that either account is mistaken. Where Greek is quoted, the Textus Receptus text served carries the same words. The record does not say that the accounts are of one meeting and does not say what the Greek verb ἅπτομαι covers beyond the words quoted. The Greek of Luke 24:39 was not fetched. The Greek New Testament wording is Tischendorf’s eighth edition as served by bolls.life (TISCH), compared with the Textus Receptus text served there (TR); neither is a critical apparatus.",
