    "text": "KJV Romans 2:13: “the doers of the law shall be justified”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Romans 2:13: οἱ ποιηταὶ … δικαιωθήσονται. KJV Romans 3:20: “by the deeds of the law there shall no flesh be justified in his sight”. Greek New Testament (Tischendorf, eighth edition) Romans 3:20: ἐξ ἔργων νόμου οὐ δικαιωθήσεται πᾶσα σὰρξ ἐνώπιον αὐτοῦ. Fetched from https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/45/2/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/45/2/, https://bolls.life/get-text/TISCH/45/3/ and https://bolls.life/get-text/TR/45/3/.",
    "reasoning": "Both verses use the Greek verb for “be justified” with the law and with doing, and one says the doers of the law will be justified while the other says no flesh will be justified by the works of the law. The two can stand together if 2:13 states what the law’s own standard would require (and 3:9–20 goes on to say that all are under sin), or if 2:13 speaks of a different kind of doer; neither verse says so in its own words.",
